* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
# Retained publisher bodies are EXACT BYTES. Their digests are asserted in tests and one is even
# its own filename, so a single rewritten line ending makes a fixture that is not the bytes it
# names, and every digest assertion against it silently wrong. The "* text=auto eol=lf" default
# above is wrong for them: git resolves .bin as "text: auto", so it applies its own content
# heuristic per file rather than any declaration here. A body containing NUL is seen as binary and
# is then safe by accident; a NUL-free body is seen as TEXT, the eol filter runs over it, and its
# CRLF are rewritten. That is not hypothetical. The tracked Akoma Ntoso body lu-xml-200-body.bin is
# NUL-free AND carries 69 CRLF, so the "*.bin binary" rule below is what keeps it equal to the
# publisher bytes its digest names. For that file the rule is repair, not prevention.
# The incident that proved this: staging that body without a binary declaration rewrote its CRLF
# and changed its digest from 9e43a99e.. to fc3303d4.., which would have committed a fixture that
# is not the publisher bytes it names. That was first fixed with a lane-scoped path rule; this
# general *.bin rule supersedes it, so the scoped line is gone and one rule covers every publisher
# body.
# THIS PARAGRAPH DELIBERATELY CARRIES NO FILE CENSUS. Both earlier versions carried one, and both
# concluded that the NUL-free bodies were safe because none of them contained CRLF. That was
# already untrue of lu-xml-200-body.bin, whose digest the incident above records, so the census
# was not merely stale, it contradicted the incident recorded beside it. A count is also wrong
# again at the next fixture. The rule holds for every .bin whatever a census says, which is
# exactly why it is declared rather than reasoned about per file.
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
